# Exercise: Integer Power
#
# `i.pow` is the builtin for integer exponentiation. Like `i./` and
# `i.%` from earlier exercises, it returns a success Bool on top of
# the result:
#
#   Stack effect: ( base exp -- result Bool )
#
# The Bool is `false` for negative exponents, exponents larger than
# u32::MAX, or arithmetic overflow. `0^0 = 1` with Bool=true.
#
# Examples (drop the Bool when you know the inputs are safe):
#   2 3 i.pow drop      # 8        — 2^3
#   5 0 i.pow drop      # 1        — 5^0
#   10 2 i.pow drop     # 100      — 10^2
#
# Note: `i.pow` is a builtin; it is NOT brought in by `include
# std:imath` the way `abs`/`min`/`max`/`gcd`/`clamp` are — so
# this file doesn't include std:imath at all. The bare name
# `pow` does not currently resolve (patch-seq #489). Other
# integer ops like `+`, `-`, `*`, `<`, `>` *do* resolve unprefixed
# via sugar — `pow` is just an outlier until that ships.
#
# Your task: Calculate powers of 2.
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

# seq:allow(unchecked-i-pow)
: power-of-2 ( Int -- Int )
    # Calculate 2^n. Drop the success Bool from i.pow.
    # Your code here
    drop 0
;

# Do not edit below this line

: test-pow ( -- )
    3 4 i.pow                 # ( 81 true )
    swap 81 test.assert-eq    # ( true ) — checks result
    test.assert               # consumes the success Bool
;

: test-power-of-2 ( -- )
    8 power-of-2
    256 test.assert-eq
;

: test-power-zero ( -- )
    0 power-of-2
    1 test.assert-eq
;
